1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:03,033
Here's a sudden question for you

2
00:00:03,600 --> 00:00:08,200
Do you believe atoms really exist?

3
00:00:08,733 --> 00:00:11,833
"Of course they do!" you might think, right?

4
00:00:12,400 --> 00:00:16,233
But actually, just over 120 years ago,

5
00:00:16,466 --> 00:00:19,933
even among scientists, "Atoms don't exist!"

6
00:00:20,466 --> 00:00:23,066
"They're just a convenient calculation tool!"

7
00:00:23,300 --> 00:00:25,533
was the prevailing opinion

8
00:00:26,400 --> 00:00:31,500
Today, we'll explore how the mysterious motion of tiny particles released from pollen floating on water

9
00:00:31,900 --> 00:00:35,166
proved the existence of atoms

10
00:00:35,566 --> 00:00:38,666
Let me tell you this amazing story

11
00:00:41,766 --> 00:00:44,633
Let's start with the historical background

12
00:00:45,533 --> 00:00:50,566
At the end of the 19th century, the scientific world was in the midst of a great debate

13
00:00:51,500 --> 00:00:56,700
On one side were the atomists, like Boltzmann, who argued:

14
00:00:58,100 --> 00:01:06,466
"Matter is made of invisible tiny particles—what we now call atoms and molecules—

15
00:01:06,466 --> 00:01:12,700
and heat is the motion of these molecules"

16
00:01:13,266 --> 00:01:16,066
That's what the atomists believed

17
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:19,800
But the opposition was also powerful

18
00:01:20,600 --> 00:01:23,766
The energeticists, Mach and Ostwald, argued:

19
00:01:24,633 --> 00:01:28,766
"Atoms are merely a convenient concept for calculations"

20
00:01:29,333 --> 00:01:36,300
"How can you claim something exists if you can't directly observe it!"

21
00:01:37,733 --> 00:01:43,766
In fact, Ernst Mach, one of the energeticists, stated clearly:

22
00:01:44,500 --> 00:01:49,866
"I do not believe in atoms; they are merely an artificial, useful device"

23
00:01:50,533 --> 00:01:55,333
Hmm, when you think about it, their argument makes sense, doesn't it?

24
00:01:56,033 --> 00:02:01,000
Indeed, no one had ever directly 'seen' an atom

25
00:02:02,066 --> 00:02:06,400
Let's go back a bit in time to 1827

26
00:02:07,200 --> 00:02:12,800
British botanist Robert Brown discovered a strange phenomenon

27
00:02:13,833 --> 00:02:18,033
When he placed pollen on water and observed it under a microscope,

28
00:02:18,733 --> 00:02:23,400
tiny particles released from the pollen kept moving in zigzag patterns on their own

29
00:02:24,033 --> 00:02:26,133
As if they were alive

30
00:02:26,966 --> 00:02:29,900
Is it because the pollen is alive?

31
00:02:30,633 --> 00:02:32,700
Brown initially thought so

32
00:02:33,833 --> 00:02:39,100
But even pollen from specimens over 100 years old moved the same way

33
00:02:40,300 --> 00:02:42,000
It wasn't life force

34
00:02:42,633 --> 00:02:45,833
Then is it special because it's organic matter?

35
00:02:46,700 --> 00:02:51,833
He experimented with particles of coal, rock, and metal

36
00:02:52,500 --> 00:02:56,466
Remarkably, even particles scraped from the Sphinx moved the same way

37
00:02:57,166 --> 00:02:59,500
Whether it's organic or not doesn't matter

38
00:03:00,233 --> 00:03:03,800
After that, various hypotheses were proposed

39
00:03:04,400 --> 00:03:08,766
The particles moved in random directions, so it wasn't convection

40
00:03:09,633 --> 00:03:13,666
They weren't attracted to electrodes, so it wasn't electrical force

41
00:03:14,433 --> 00:03:19,533
The motion didn't weaken even after two years of observation,

42
00:03:19,533 --> 00:03:22,866
so it wasn't a chemical reaction like a camphor boat

43
00:03:23,600 --> 00:03:31,966
For about 80 years after the 1827 discovery, no one could explain this mysterious motion

44
00:03:33,500 --> 00:03:39,866
In 1888, French physicist Louis Georges Gouy

45
00:03:40,266 --> 00:03:48,900
made a crucial insight: "Brownian motion cannot be explained by the second law of thermodynamics"

46
00:03:49,866 --> 00:03:57,033
Today we understand that Brownian motion doesn't contradict the second law of thermodynamics,

47
00:03:57,466 --> 00:04:05,333
but indeed, "particles that keep moving forever" might seem like a perpetual motion machine

48
00:04:06,733 --> 00:04:11,833
In 1905, a 26-year-old Einstein enters the scene

49
00:04:12,733 --> 00:04:20,000
Einstein had read both Boltzmann's and Mach's books, learned about the atomic debate,

50
00:04:20,333 --> 00:04:24,166
and wanted to prove the existence of atoms

51
00:04:24,766 --> 00:04:32,233
That year, he published five important papers that fundamentally changed physics

52
00:04:33,300 --> 00:04:45,000
The theory of the photoelectric effect, special relativity, the famous E=mc² equation showing mass-energy equivalence,

53
00:04:45,666 --> 00:04:50,533
and today's main topic—the theory of Brownian motion

54
00:04:51,733 --> 00:04:55,833
Any single one of these would have made history,

55
00:04:56,166 --> 00:04:59,366
yet he published them all in succession within one year

56
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:05,533
1905 later became known as the "Miracle Year"

57
00:05:07,100 --> 00:05:09,900
Einstein's approach was revolutionary

58
00:05:11,133 --> 00:05:17,833
Previous approaches tried to track the movement of individual particles moving in random directions

59
00:05:19,100 --> 00:05:26,733
But just tracking individual particles only revealed that the motion was irregular—nothing more

60
00:05:28,166 --> 00:05:32,133
But Einstein focused not on individual movements,

61
00:05:32,133 --> 00:05:36,800
but on statistical behavior

62
00:05:37,833 --> 00:05:39,966
This is similar to coin flipping

63
00:05:40,733 --> 00:05:42,900
A single result is unpredictable

64
00:05:43,600 --> 00:05:48,966
Heads or tails is 50-50, so you can only guess

65
00:05:49,633 --> 00:05:56,233
But if you flip a million times, you can predict it'll be roughly half and half, right?

66
00:05:57,133 --> 00:06:02,833
Similarly, while you can't know how a single particle will move in the next instant,

67
00:06:03,266 --> 00:06:07,233
you should be able to predict how far it will travel over time

68
00:06:08,333 --> 00:06:14,000
Einstein connected Fick's law—the macroscopic diffusion law—

69
00:06:14,400 --> 00:06:21,566
with Stokes' law—the microscopic viscous resistance—through thermal energy

70
00:06:22,800 --> 00:06:26,433
And this is the equation he derived

71
00:06:27,533 --> 00:06:34,666
It was the moment when the invisible world of atoms was connected to visible particle motion through temperature

72
00:06:35,600 --> 00:06:39,400
Einstein then had another brilliant insight

73
00:06:40,300 --> 00:06:46,366
Traditionally, the diffusion equation described how many particles spread out

74
00:06:47,366 --> 00:06:49,500
But Einstein reinterpreted it

75
00:06:50,066 --> 00:06:57,266
as a probability question: "Where is a single particle most likely to be?"

76
00:06:58,200 --> 00:07:00,833
This shift in perspective was key

77
00:07:01,900 --> 00:07:04,833
Since a particle moves right and left,

78
00:07:05,433 --> 00:07:08,300
the average of its displacement becomes zero

79
00:07:09,100 --> 00:07:11,700
The positives and negatives cancel out

80
00:07:12,700 --> 00:07:15,733
But what about the average of the squared distance?

81
00:07:16,466 --> 00:07:20,133
Squaring always gives a positive value,

82
00:07:20,533 --> 00:07:23,800
so the positives and negatives don't cancel out

83
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:27,600
And this is the equation Einstein derived

84
00:07:27,833 --> 00:07:32,200
for the mean squared displacement of Brownian motion

85
00:07:33,233 --> 00:07:34,733
This is amazing!

86
00:07:35,366 --> 00:07:40,000
The mean squared displacement is proportional to time

87
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:48,033
Calculating specifically, the distance traveled in 30 seconds is about 4.3 μm

88
00:07:48,766 --> 00:07:53,933
This was large enough to be observed with microscopes of that era

89
00:07:54,700 --> 00:07:56,233
Think about it

90
00:07:57,133 --> 00:08:03,333
Atoms are about 0.1 to 0.3 nm in size

91
00:08:04,066 --> 00:08:10,233
Yet they became observable in the micrometer world, tens of thousands of times larger

92
00:08:11,533 --> 00:08:14,900
Einstein wrote at the end of his paper:

93
00:08:15,933 --> 00:08:21,133
"I hope that some researcher will soon undertake to solve this problem"

94
00:08:21,933 --> 00:08:28,400
"If this experiment succeeds, it would provide new and powerful evidence for atomic theory"

95
00:08:29,333 --> 00:08:35,833
In other words, "Could someone good at experiments please verify this!"

96
00:08:36,933 --> 00:08:42,233
The French physicist Jean Perrin took up that challenge

97
00:08:43,366 --> 00:08:45,966
But the experiments were extremely difficult

98
00:08:47,033 --> 00:08:53,100
To verify the theory, the particles needed to be perfectly uniform in size

99
00:08:53,666 --> 00:08:56,666
Perrin's ingenuity was remarkable

100
00:08:57,533 --> 00:09:05,166
He created particles of gamboge—a yellow resin used in paints—through three stages of centrifugation

101
00:09:05,466 --> 00:09:10,433
He prepared particles uniformly sized at 0.3 μm

102
00:09:10,766 --> 00:09:15,566
What Perrin observed was a phenomenon called sedimentation equilibrium

103
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:20,166
Air becomes thinner at high mountain altitudes, right?

104
00:09:20,800 --> 00:09:24,900
This was already known in the 19th century,

105
00:09:25,400 --> 00:09:30,133
and Boltzmann explained it as air molecules being pulled by Earth's gravity,

106
00:09:30,500 --> 00:09:35,466
becoming denser near the surface and thinner at higher altitudes

107
00:09:36,300 --> 00:09:39,700
But since air molecules couldn't be seen,

108
00:09:40,166 --> 00:09:43,466
his idea couldn't be verified

109
00:09:44,433 --> 00:09:49,666
However, Perrin, by observing particles in water under a microscope,

110
00:09:50,266 --> 00:09:57,033
attempted to verify both sedimentation equilibrium and Einstein's theory of Brownian motion

111
00:09:57,800 --> 00:10:01,166
Then came days of painstaking experiments and observations

112
00:10:02,100 --> 00:10:06,900
He tracked particles under the microscope and recorded their positions at regular intervals

113
00:10:07,733 --> 00:10:09,900
Again and again

114
00:10:10,666 --> 00:10:12,533
And the results?

115
00:10:14,100 --> 00:10:17,966
They matched Einstein's theory exactly!

116
00:10:18,466 --> 00:10:22,900
The mean squared displacement was indeed proportional to time

117
00:10:23,833 --> 00:10:28,933
Upon learning of these results, Einstein sent a letter of thanks to Perrin

118
00:10:29,800 --> 00:10:35,433
"I thought it would be impossible to measure Brownian motion with such precision"

119
00:10:36,200 --> 00:10:40,000
"It was fortunate that you took on this work"

120
00:10:41,300 --> 00:10:45,466
The very person who proposed the theory thought the experiment would be too difficult,

121
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:48,266
yet Perrin accomplished it brilliantly

122
00:10:49,100 --> 00:10:53,200
Perrin's experiments yielded something even more important

123
00:10:53,966 --> 00:10:57,366
By substituting experimental data into Einstein's equation,

124
00:10:57,866 --> 00:10:59,866
the Boltzmann constant could be determined

125
00:11:00,833 --> 00:11:05,933
And by dividing the gas constant R by the Boltzmann constant,

126
00:11:07,200 --> 00:11:12,566
NA = 6.5 × 10^23

127
00:11:13,866 --> 00:11:17,966
This is the number of particles in one mole of a substance—

128
00:11:18,533 --> 00:11:19,866
Avogadro's number

129
00:11:21,033 --> 00:11:28,266
Today, Avogadro's number is known to be approximately 6.02 × 10^23,

130
00:11:28,866 --> 00:11:32,233
but for that time, this was an extremely accurate measurement

131
00:11:33,333 --> 00:11:39,533
Remarkably, this value matched those obtained by completely different methods

132
00:11:40,366 --> 00:11:43,700
Planck's blackbody radiation, radioactive decay,

133
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:47,566
Millikan's oil drop experiment, X-ray diffraction—

134
00:11:47,833 --> 00:11:52,966
Avogadro's number obtained by various methods all agreed

135
00:11:53,533 --> 00:11:57,233
This can't be explained by coincidence, can it?

136
00:11:58,333 --> 00:12:04,433
In 1909, Ostwald, who had once denied atoms, conceded

137
00:12:05,900 --> 00:12:13,166
"I am convinced that we have recently obtained experimental evidence for the atomic hypothesis"

138
00:12:14,066 --> 00:12:18,400
"I no longer have any reason to oppose the atomic hypothesis"

139
00:12:19,333 --> 00:12:24,800
In 1926, Perrin was awarded the Nobel Prize in Physics

140
00:12:25,466 --> 00:12:33,366
The citation read: "For his work on the discontinuous structure of matter, especially for his discovery of sedimentation equilibrium"

141
00:12:34,533 --> 00:12:42,633
The discontinuous structure of matter refers to the idea that matter is composed of small particles called atoms

142
00:12:43,733 --> 00:12:48,200
The atomic debate ended in victory for the atomists

143
00:12:49,333 --> 00:12:56,866
But we shouldn't mock the energeticists as stubborn people who refused to accept new ideas

144
00:12:57,766 --> 00:13:05,166
They were simply being faithful to Occam's Razor: "One should not assume what cannot be observed"

145
00:13:06,100 --> 00:13:09,866
Their attitude was scientifically correct

146
00:13:10,866 --> 00:13:16,466
What made Einstein and Perrin remarkable was that they made the invisible visible

147
00:13:17,433 --> 00:13:22,500
They demonstrated the existence of atoms in a way that had previously been impossible

148
00:13:25,500 --> 00:13:30,300
Brownian motion was discovered in 1827,

149
00:13:30,700 --> 00:13:36,100
and for about 80 years, the mysterious motion of tiny particles remained unexplained

150
00:13:36,866 --> 00:13:40,166
Einstein was the one who solved it theoretically

151
00:13:41,466 --> 00:13:43,633
Using statistical mechanics,

152
00:13:43,833 --> 00:13:49,100
he built a theory connecting visible particles to invisible atoms

153
00:13:49,966 --> 00:13:54,133
And Perrin, through persistent experimentation, verified the theory

154
00:13:54,666 --> 00:13:57,566
and put an end to the atomic debate

155
00:13:58,766 --> 00:14:03,033
The existence of atoms that we take for granted today—

156
00:14:03,566 --> 00:14:07,566
before it became a scientifically grounded, empirically proven existence,

157
00:14:08,033 --> 00:14:12,700
it required the accumulated efforts and ingenuity of many people

158
00:14:13,866 --> 00:14:16,400
Making the invisible visible—

159
00:14:17,200 --> 00:14:22,600
more recent examples include neutrino oscillations and gravitational waves

160
00:14:23,500 --> 00:14:27,466
I hope some of that romance of science has come through

161
00:14:28,333 --> 00:14:31,933
Until the next video, take care!
